{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current DailyMed label.","fda_source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%22aa610e56-1d1d-11e1-8bc2-0800200c9a66%22&limit=5","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2018/202810s010lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf"],"proposed_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current FDA/openFDA label.","rationale":"The no-boxed-warning conclusion is supportable from FDA labeling/openFDA, but the current value and source rely on DailyMed, which is not permissible for this field.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"oxcarbazepine; trade_names=Oxtellar XR; Trileptal; available_in_us=Yes; year_fda_cleared=2000","field":"generic_name; trade_names; available_in_us; year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"No change. Do not add \"Oxcarbazepine\" as a trade name; it is the generic/product name, not a brand alias.","rationale":"FDA labels and NCBI LiverTox support oxcarbazepine, Trileptal, Oxtellar XR, US approval in 2000, and US availability.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2018/202810s010lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548414/"]},{"current_value":"epilepsy_type=Focal; filter_epilepsy_type=Focal; status_or_notes=Current ASM for focal/partial-onset seizures.","field":"epilepsy_type; filter_epilepsy_type; status_or_notes","proposed_value":"No change.","rationale":"FDA labeling states Trileptal/Oxtellar XR are indicated for partial-onset seizures, corresponding to focal seizures.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2018/202810s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"formulations_available=Tablet; oral suspension; extended-release tablet; filter_formulation=Liquid; Long acting; Tablet; maximum_approved_daily_dose=2400 mg/day; typical_doses_per_day=Adults: 1200-2400 mg/day; IR divided BID, ER once daily","field":"formulations_available; filter_formulation; maximum_approved_daily_dose; typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"No change.","rationale":"FDA labels support film-coated tablets, oral suspension, ER tablets, adult dosing up to 2400 mg/day, IR BID administration, and ER once-daily administration.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2018/202810s010lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482313/"]},{"current_value":"1200 mg/day common adult maintenance target","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"600 mg/day adjunctive minimum effective dosage in Barcs2000; 1200 mg/day common adult target/maintenance dose in FDA labeling","rationale":"Barcs2000 explicitly reports 600 mg/day as the minimum effective dosage in adjunctive therapy; FDA labeling supports 1200 mg/day as a common recommended/effective adult dose.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11114219/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"CNS: dizziness 22-49%, CNS: somnolence 19-28%, GI: nausea 15%, GI: vomiting 13%, neurologic: diplopia 14%, metabolic: hyponatremia 2-3%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: dizziness 20-49%, CNS: somnolence 12-36%, GI: nausea 15-29%, GI: vomiting 6-36%, neurologic/eye: diplopia 10-40%, metabolic: hyponatremia 1-5% (clinically significant sodium <125 mmol/L 2.5% in controlled epilepsy studies)","rationale":"Current values understate several FDA-label active-dose adverse-reaction ranges, especially somnolence, nausea, vomiting, and diplopia. Oxtellar XR and Trileptal labels support the proposed active-dose ranges.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2018/202810s010lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Induces CYP3A4/UGT; inhibits CYP2C19; weaker inducer than carbamazepine; filter_enzyme_effect=Inhibitor; Weak inducer","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting; filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"No change.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports CYP3A4/5 induction, clinically relevant CYP2C19 inhibition, and weak UGT induction; NCBI sources support weak induction and less induction than carbamazepine.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482313/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548414/"]},{"current_value":"Precise mechanism is unknown; active MHD metabolite blocks voltage-sensitive sodium channels, stabilizes hyperexcited neural membranes, and may also increase potassium conductance and modulate high-voltage calcium channels.; mechanism_confidence=Moderate; mechanism_source_tier=FDA label","field":"mechanism_of_action; mechanism_confidence; mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"No change.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports the unknown precise mechanism, primary MHD activity, voltage-sensitive sodium-channel blockade, possible potassium-conductance increase, and high-voltage calcium-channel modulation.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Calcium channel / alpha-2-delta; Potassium channel","field":"filter_mechanism","proposed_value":"Sodium channel; Potassium channel; Calcium channel","rationale":"FDA labeling supports voltage-sensitive sodium-channel blockade as the primary described mechanism and possible high-voltage calcium-channel modulation; it does not support alpha-2-delta binding.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482313/"]},{"current_value":"half_life_range=Parent ~2 h; active MHD ~8-15 h; major_organ_for_metabolism=Liver cytosolic reduction/glucuronidation; renal excretion","field":"half_life_range; major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"No change, though a tighter FDA-label wording would be: Parent about 2 h; active MHD about 9 h (IR) and about 9-11 h for Oxtellar XR.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports parent half-life about 2 hours, MHD about 9 hours, hepatic cytosolic reduction/glucuronidation, and predominant renal excretion of metabolites. NCBI StatPearls supports parent 1-3.7 h and active metabolite 8-10 h.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2018/202810s010lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482313/"]},{"current_value":"Liver/hepatic; Renal/no major metabolism","field":"filter_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver/hepatic; Renal excretion","rationale":"FDA labeling states oxcarbazepine is extensively metabolized and then predominantly excreted by the kidneys; the phrase \"Renal/no major metabolism\" is misleading for oxcarbazepine.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482313/"]},{"current_value":"No clinically meaningful QT effect established; filter_qt_effect=No known meaningful QT effect","field":"qt_interval_effect; filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"No dedicated therapeutic QT-effect statement found in reviewed FDA labeling; QT prolongation is listed among overdose events.","rationale":"Reviewed FDA labeling does not establish a therapeutic clinically meaningful QT effect, but Oxtellar XR labeling lists QT prolongation among isolated overdose events, so the current wording needs a source-qualified caveat.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2018/202810s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"alternate_generic_names=; pubmed_search_aliases=","field":"alternate_generic_names; pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"alternate_generic_names: \"\"; pubmed_search_aliases: \"OXC; OXC XR; SPN-804; TRI476\"","rationale":"No separate alternate generic name is needed, but PubMed/trial search aliases should include OXC/OXC XR and development codes SPN-804 and TRI476 to prevent missed oxcarbazepine trial records.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24359313/","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00772603","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00975715"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Society ASM list; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources; mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata labeling; FDA/openFDA labeling; NCBI LiverTox; NCBI StatPearls; PubMed RCT abstracts; ClinicalTrials.gov","rationale":"Epilepsy Society and Epilepsy Foundation Australia are not in the requested trusted-source domain list, and DailyMed is not acceptable for the black-box source. Row facts are better supported by FDA/accessdata/openFDA, NCBI, PubMed, and ClinicalTrials.gov.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021014s050lbl.pdf